<section class="essay-section essay-continuation" id="preparing-states"><div class="prose">
 <h2>What do successive measurements build?</h2>
 <p>We start with no correlations: each qubit has state <Math tex="I/2"/>. For two qubits, a singlet–triplet measurement has the following branches:</p>
 <Math display tex={String.raw`\frac{I}{4}\longrightarrow\begin{cases}P_s & \text{with probability }1/4,\\[2pt]P_t/3 & \text{with probability }3/4.\end{cases}`}/>
 <p>The singlet branch is a pure entangled state. The triplet branch is still mixed: it is uniform on a three-dimensional subspace. A second test of the same pair returns triplet with certainty and leaves that state unchanged. Learning the spin label does not select a pure state within the corresponding space.</p>
 <p>In fact, this mixed triplet is separable. Choose a pure qubit state uniformly over directions, prepare two copies, then forget the direction. Averaging those product states gives exactly <Math tex="P_t/3"/>. The triplet space contains entangled states, but the particular state prepared from mixed input has no entanglement. If we forget the measurement outcome as well, the two branches average back to <Math tex="I/4"/>.</p>
 <h3>Symmetric blocks and overlapping measurements</h3>
 <p>The same construction works for a larger block of <Math tex="k"/> qubits:</p>
 <Math display tex={String.raw`\frac{P_{\mathrm{sym},k}}{k+1}=\int d\psi\,\bigl(|\psi\rangle\langle\psi|\bigr)^{\otimes k}.`}/>
 <p>The measure is normalized and uniform over pure qubit states. The integral is supported on the symmetric spin-<Math tex="k/2"/> irrep and is invariant under collective rotations; these facts and trace one determine the normalized projector. It is a mixture of product states, even though being fully symmetric does not by itself imply separability.</p>
 <p>Every internal pair in this block is triplet. To change it using STP, we need to interact it with something outside the block. Preparing two symmetric blocks independently does not make their union fully symmetric. An overlapping measurement can change their internal correlations and entangle systems across the old block boundaries.</p>
 <p>Prepare simple blocks independently, then measure a pair that crosses their boundaries. Starting with two singlets, either outcome on a cross-pair gives a useful state: new singlet pairs, or two spin-one pairs entangled into total spin zero. Starting with two mixed triplets can also create entanglement. The output need not remain a collection of the original blocks.</p>
</div></section>
